</ul><h1>Light The Night</h1><p>Maybe a decade or two ago, the sales pitch for these outdoor lighting timers would be: Hey, get them in September for cheap and be ready so you can go full-Griswold come December!</p>
<p>But it’s 2026. Which means, at least in some neighborhoods, Halloween decorations are more in style than Christmas lights. Hence why now is the perfect time to get these, as we stand on the precipice of early fall, aka spooky season.</p>
<p>But if we may raise one issue: these Halloween decorations? They lack creativity. Sure, a few Home Depot skeletons are fun, but so many people just set them up and call it a day. It’s so lazy! And what’s more, these decorations are so ubiquitous at this point, they barely register as scary anymore.</p>
<p>We say, this year, why not set up your skeletons in a way that tells a truly terrifying tale?</p>
<p>Here are a few absolutely frightening tableaus we’ve come up with:</p>
<p><strong>“Your thoughts on the reading?”</strong></p>
<p>Several enormous skeletons sit around a table, with books and notebooks piled next to each one, clearly a college seminar. At the head sits another skeleton (this one wearing glasses and/or a tweed blazer with elbow patches to identify it as the skeleton professor) extending a bony finger toward one student, calling on them to contribute. The student in question, however, has not finished the reading and is now desperately trying to come up with something profound to say about the first chapter.</p>
<p><strong>The outpouring goes unacknowledged</strong></p>
<p>A skeleton stands in the middle of the yard, looking at a phone. On the screen is a long, heartfelt message the skeleton has composed and sent, one that clearly took a long time and caused a great deal of anxiety. At the bottom corner of the message, the recipient’s only reply: a thumbs-up reaction. The skeleton wonders what to make of this. Could it be an earnest ‘I totally agree,’ or is it a sarcastic ‘sure, buddy, whatever you say’?</p>
<p><strong>The sneeze</strong></p>
<p>A skeleton holds its hand up to its nasal cavity, having just sneezed. And the sneeze in question was one of those that indicate an oncoming cold. Pinned to a tree behind the skeleton: a calendar. The days have been crossed off up to a point. Then, there sits an unmarked gap (today) before a square in which the skeleton has written: “leave on vacation.”</p>
<p><strong>“Who’s your friend?”</strong></p>
<p>Two skeletons, let’s call them A and B, stand in casual conversation, or so it looks. But underneath the facade of lighthearted banter runs a vein of terror. You see, A has met B before, but can’t recall his name, and they’ve progressed too far into the conversation; asking now would be inappropriate. So far, it hasn’t been an issue. But here comes a third skeleton, C, a friend of A’s, sauntering over, likely expecting an introduction.</p>
<p>See what we mean? Don’t these scenes make your skin crawl? And you can light them easily with these outdoor timers!</p>